{"id":"neet-2026r-q049","pn":49,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Electrode potential from Gibbs energy","q":"The standard electrode potential (E°) for the half-cell reaction Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺ at 298 K is\n(Given : E°(Fe³⁺/Fe) = –0.04 V and E°(Fe²⁺/Fe) = –0.44 V at 298 K)","o":["+0.76 V","–0.48 V","+0.92 V","+0.40 V"],"c":0,"s":"Fe³⁺ → Fe (3 electrons, –0.04 V) is the sum of Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺ (1 electron, E° unknown) and Fe²⁺ → Fe (2 electrons, –0.44 V).\nSo ΔG°(Fe³⁺/Fe) = ΔG°(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) + ΔG°(Fe²⁺/Fe).\nUse ΔG° = –nFE°: –3F(–0.04) = –1F(E°) + (–2F)(–0.44).\n0.12 = –E° + 0.88.\nE° = 0.88 – 0.12 = 0.76 V.","sfig":{"w":240,"h":116,"alt":"Cycle: Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺ with E° marked ?, Fe²⁺ to Fe marked –0.44 V, and Fe³⁺ directly to Fe marked –0.04 V.","d":[["t",30,66,"Fe³⁺"],["l",64,62,104,62,"a"],["t",110,66,"Fe²⁺"],["l",144,62,184,62,"a"],["t",190,66,"Fe"],["p","M42 52 V28 H120 V50","a"],["t",81,22,"?","m"],["p","M134 52 V28 H198 V50","a"],["t",166,22,"–0.44 V","m"],["p","M42 70 V92 H198 V72","a"],["t",120,108,"–0.04 V","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q051","pn":51,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Kohlrausch law limiting conductivity","q":"For a salt XY, which is a strong electrolyte, the plot of Λm versus √c has a slope of –90.0 S cm² mol⁻³ᐟ² L¹ᐟ² at 298 K. At 0.01 M concentration of XY, the value of Λm is 145.0 S cm² mol⁻¹. The limiting molar conductivity of Y⁻ ion (λ°Y⁻, in S cm² mol⁻¹) at 298 K will be\n(Given : λ°X⁺ = 74.0 S cm² mol⁻¹)","o":["100.0","90.0","76.0","80.0"],"c":3,"s":"Debye-Huckel-Onsager: Λm = Λ°m – A√c, a straight line with slope –A = –90.\n√0.01 = 0.1, so 145 = Λ°m – 90 × 0.1.\nΛ°m = 154 S cm² mol⁻¹.\nΛ°m(XY) = λ°X⁺ + λ°Y⁻, so 154 = 74 + λ°Y⁻.\nλ°Y⁻ = 80 S cm² mol⁻¹."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q055","pn":55,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Nernst equation","q":"Calculate emf of the half cell given below:\nPt(s) | H₂(g, 2 atm) | HCl(aq, 0.02 M)\nE°(H₂/H⁺) = 0\n(Given: 2.303RT/F = 0.059, log 2 = 0.3010)","o":["0.109 V","0.035 V","–0.035 V","–0.109 V"],"c":0,"s":"The half-cell reaction is H₂(g) → 2H⁺(aq) + 2e⁻.\nE = E° – (2.303RT/nF) log([H⁺]²/P_H₂).\n[H⁺] = 0.02 M (from HCl), P_H₂ = 2 atm, 2.303RT/F = 0.059, n = 2.\nE = 0 – (0.059/2) log((0.02)²/2).\n= –0.0295 log(0.0004/2) = –0.0295 log(2×10⁻⁴).\n= –0.0295 (log2 + log10⁻⁴) = –0.0295 (0.301 – 4).\n= +0.109 V."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q073","pn":73,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Faraday's laws","q":"A solution of copper sulphate is electrolysed for 10 minutes with a current of 1.5 amperes. The mass of copper deposited at cathode is :\n(Given : Molar mass of Cu = 63 g mol⁻¹; 1 F = 96487 C mol⁻¹)","o":["2.4036 g","1.7018 g","0.5876 g","0.2938 g"],"c":3,"s":"CuSO₄ → Cu²⁺ + SO₄²⁻.\nAt the cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu(s), so equivalent mass E = 63/2.\nW = Z i t = (E × i × t)/(96500).\nW = (63 × 1.5 × 10 × 60)/(2 × 96500) = 56,700/193,000 = 0.2938 g."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q061","pn":61,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Molar conductivity and degree of dissociation","q":"If the molar conductivity (Λm) of a 0.050 mol L⁻¹ solution of a monobasic weak acid is 90 S cm² mol⁻¹, its extent (degree) of dissociation will be\n[Assume Λ°₊ = 349.6 S cm² mol⁻¹ and Λ°₋ = 50.4 S cm² mol⁻¹.]","o":["0.115","0.125","0.225","0.215"],"c":2,"s":"Degree of dissociation α = Λm/Λ°m.\nΛ°m = Λ°₊ + Λ°₋ = 349.6 + 50.4 = 400 S cm² mol⁻¹.\nα = 90/400 = 0.225."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q051","pn":51,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Faraday's laws of electrolysis","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Conversion) — List-II (Number of Faraday required)\nA. 1 mol of H₂O to O₂ — I. 3F\nB. 1 mol of MnO₄⁻ to Mn²⁺ — II. 2F\nC. 1.5 mol of Ca from molten CaCl₂ — III. 1F\nD. 1 mol of FeO to Fe₂O₃ — IV. 5F\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"c":2,"s":"A: 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻. For 2 mol of H₂O, 4F is needed.\nSo for 1 mol of H₂O, 4F/2 = 2F is needed. A matches II.\nB: Mn goes from +7 in MnO₄⁻ to +2 in Mn²⁺, a gain of 5 electrons. So 1 mol needs 5F. B matches IV.\nC: Ca²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ca, so 1 mol of Ca needs 2F.\nFor 1.5 mol: 2 × 1.5 = 3F. C matches I.\nD: Fe goes from +2 in FeO to +3 in Fe₂O₃, a loss of 1 electron per Fe. So 1 mol of FeO needs 1F. D matches III."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q098","pn":98,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Faraday's law of electrolysis","q":"Mass in grams of copper deposited by passing 9.6487 A current through a voltmeter containing copper sulphate solution for 100 seconds is :\n(Given : Molar mass of Cu : 63 g mol⁻¹, 1F = 96487 C)","o":["31.5 g","0.0315 g","3.15 g","0.315 g"],"c":3,"s":"Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s), so n = 2.\nMass deposited w = (M × i × t)/(nF).\nw = (63 × 9.6487 × 100)/(2 × 96487).\nw = 0.315 g."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q052","pn":52,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Cell constant","q":"The conductivity of centimolar solution of KCl at 25ºC is 0.0210 ohm⁻¹ and the resistance of the cell containing the solution at 25ºC is 60 ohm. The value of cell constant is-","o":["3.34 cm⁻¹","1.34 cm⁻¹","3.28 cm⁻¹","1.26 cm⁻¹"],"c":3,"s":"Conductivity κ = conductance G × cell constant G*.\nConductance G = 1/R, so G* = κ × R.\nG* = 0.0210 × 60 = 1.26 cm⁻¹."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q081","pn":81,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Gibbs energy and cell potential","q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :\nAssertion A : In equation ΔrG = –nFEcell, value of ΔrG depends on n.\nReason R : Ecell is an intensive property and ΔrG is an extensive property.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],"c":2,"s":"In ΔrG = –nFEcell, n is the number of electrons, so nF is the charge passed. ΔrG depends on n, so A is true.\nEcell is intensive and ΔrG is extensive, so R is true.\nBut R does not explain why ΔrG depends on n."}
